    avl_issue: ampere_altra_avl_current_issue,
  },
  training: TrainingOutcomeUnobserved,
  boot: BootReachedOperatingSystem,
  observed_at: 1787427958000,
}

data srv4_observed_at_is_a_window_lower_bound_note: String = "srv4's observed_at is the LOWER BOUND OF A WINDOW, NOT A KNOWN INSTANT, and the difference is recorded because the carrier cannot express it. The srv4 Redfish population read was taken between 19:45Z and 20:15Z on 2026-08-22 and the exact minute was not recorded; 1787427958000 is 19:45:58Z, the timestamp of the correctable-ECC entry this session read first, so it is the earliest instant the read can have occurred at. srv3's value IS an instant, because that read was taken against a live boot transition where the clock mattered. EpochMs admits only a point, so a bounded observation must either fabricate a second or pick a bound and say which -- this picks the lower bound. A consumer computing an age from this row gets an OVERESTIMATE of elapsed time, never an underestimate, which is the safe direction for a staleness question. DISSOLVE-ON: an observed-at carrier that can express a bounded window, at which point this row moves into it and this note goes."

// srv2 FAILED THE SAME PART THAT THREE SIBLINGS TRAINED, AND THAT IS THE MOST INFORMATIVE ROW
// IN THIS MODULE (2026-08-22). srv1, srv3 and srv4 all POSTed on the 64 GiB Samsung population;
// srv2 could not, was left power-cycling, and was reverted to its 8 x 16 GiB Hynix set, on which
// it booted in about 2.5 minutes and returned to service with five runners.
//
// WHY THE BOOT AXIS READS RepeatingFirmwareReset RATHER THAN A TRAINING FAILURE. The BMC event
// log shows a loop: power on, then Power.Error.Blackout after 13m14s, 14m26s, and 7m35s, with
// PowerRestorePolicy=AlwaysOn restarting it each time. That is the boot axis. The TRAINING axis
// stays Unobserved, and the distinction is exactly what this module's four independent standings
// exist for: nobody read a training mask, because on this platform nothing can.
//
// THE OBSERVABILITY FINDING IS WORTH MORE THAN THE FAILURE. HostLogger captured ZERO host serial
// entries -- not during the failures, and not during srv2's subsequent SUCCESSFUL boot either.
// That control is what makes it a finding rather than a symptom: the recorder was never running,
// so "what did the firmware say while training" is unavailable BY CONSTRUCTION on this fleet, not
// merely unread. Any future attempt is blind in the same way unless serial capture is fixed
// first, and a binary search over DIMMs would be running on pass/fail and wall-clock alone.
//
// WHAT THE EVIDENCE RULES OUT, recorded so it is not re-investigated: the 2026-06-02 aux-connector
// VRM failure did NOT recur (all 16 rails matched srv1 within millivolts, including the five that
// read 0.01 V then), and the SoC was neither dead nor idle -- all eight per-DIMM thermal sensors
// read 67-75 C against a 50 C SoC, matching a fully-booted srv1, an hour after srv2 last ran an
// OS. The SoC was alive and driving all eight channels. The thermal spread was EVEN, so no
// channel was being skipped: this was a convergence failure across a populated bus, not one dead
// channel.
//
// VARIABLE TIME-TO-FAILURE IS THE REASON THIS IS NOT CLOSED. 13m14s, 14m26s and 7m35s are not a
// deterministic firmware timeout; they point at something marginal -- signal integrity or a flaky
// rank. The practical consequence is that ONE successful boot would not establish a fix, so the
// part_qualification axis below stays NotListedInVendorAvl and this host's standing stays a
// failure rather than being softened by the three siblings that worked.
data srv2_failed_64gib_population_standing: HostMemoryStanding = HostMemoryStanding {
  host: operator_host_srv2,
  part_number: "M393A8G40D40-CRB0Q",
  population: ["DDR4_A1", "DDR4_B1", "DDR4_C1", "DDR4_D1", "DDR4_E1", "DDR4_F1", "DDR4_G1", "DDR4_H1"],
  class_support: ClassSupportedByBoard,
  part_qualification: NotListedInVendorAvl {
    avl_authority: ampere_altra_avl_authority,
    avl_issue: ampere_altra_avl_current_issue,
  },
  training: TrainingOutcomeUnobserved,
  boot: RepeatingFirmwareReset { token: "Power.Error.Blackout (no firmware POST token reached; BootProgress remained Unspecified at epoch zero)", cycles: 3 },
  observed_at: 1787436400000,
}

data srv2_boot_serial_unobservable_note: String = "srv2 HostLogger held ZERO entries across both the failed 64 GiB cycles AND the successful 8 x 16 GiB boot that followed. The successful boot is the control: it proves the absence is a missing recorder, not a mute SoC. Consequence for any retry: the training axis cannot be observed on this fleet until host serial capture works (the obmc-console server configs for ttyS1/ttyS2/ttyS3 exist -- ttyS2 is the SCP, which runs DDR training -- but nothing is captured), so the highest-value action before re-testing 64 GiB on srv2 is fixing serial capture or attaching a physical cable. Failing that, the per-DIMM thermal sensors are the working instrument: the baseline is all eight at 67-75 C during a FAILED run, so a future attempt showing one channel cool fingers that channel with no firmware log needed."

// THE ONE OPEN ITEM ON THIS POPULATION, kept as data rather than left in a chat log.
// srv4 logged a single CORRECTABLE ECC error at 2026-08-22T19:45:57Z -- Socket 0 / Channel 0 /
// DIMM 0, bank 13 row 166 column 96 -- about three minutes after an unclean restart following a
// power blackout. Corrected, no uncorrectable event, no repeat, and no comparable event on srv3.
// It does NOT move any of the four axes: boot is established, training is operational, and a
// corrected single-bit event is not a qualification result in either direction.
// It is recorded because the distinguishing fact is a REPEAT at the same bank/row/column, which
// separates a marginal device from a single upset, and nobody can look for a repeat against an
// event that was never written down.
data srv4_correctable_ecc_watch_note: String = "srv4 dimm0 (Bank 1 DDR4_A1, serial 026B6F84): one MemoryECCCorrectable at 2026-08-22T19:45:57Z, bank 13 row 166 column 96, decoded through the BMC's own OpenBMC message registry rather than guessed. Watch for recurrence at the same coordinates. A second event there is a device-level finding; scattered single events across different addresses are not."

data platform_training_implication_note: String = "RELOCATED 2026-08-19 from the deleted gunbc.memory_part_selection, whose candidate/frontier half was a selection island; this evidence half is qualification, so it lives beside the four standings it grades. The grading rule is mechanical and derived: a candidate is graded by whether it must execute a training step THIS platform has been observed to fail, and by how strongly that failure is evidenced. For the ALTRAD8UD-1L2T the step is the DDR4 data-buffer read-phase calibration (MREP), which only load-reduced modules execute, so the grading is independent of vendor, part number, capacity, speed grade, and presence on any approved-vendor list. THE SEVERITY IS A FUNCTION OF EVIDENCE, and an earlier revision of this module got that wrong in a way worth recording: it refused every module executing the failing step, on any board, at any standing including EvidenceUnobserved. Because capacities near a generation's die-density ceiling are disproportionately load-reduced, that blanket rule removed most of the 64GB DDR4 market by construction — the exact blocklist this module's prose claimed to avoid, reached by a different route, and the opposite of letting a buyer shop the whole market. Now: a decode of this platform's own syndrome register refuses; an association carrying byte-identical controller status words, or an inference from sequence position, requires a SAMPLE before committing to a full population and stays on the Pareto frontier; an unobserved standing implicates nothing. gunbc holds MREP at AssociatedByIdenticalSignature, so the honest verdict for another ALTRAD8UD LRDIMM today is buy-one-and-test, not reject. Failures are scoped to the board they were observed on, so an ALTRAD8UD receipt cannot speak for a different platform. KNOWN GAP, stated rather than papered over: BaseboardModel currently has exactly one variant, so the board scoping is real in the code but has no executing cross-board control — minting a second board purely to test it would be a fabricated entity, and the control lands when a second real board is modeled. The second axis, topology pressure, is derived rather than observed: when a capacity cannot be reached as a dual-rank registered module within the generation's monolithic die-density ceiling, that capacity forces extra ranks or stacking, which is why 64GB DDR4 at older speed grades is disproportionately load-reduced while 32GB is not. A buyer economizing on speed grade at 64GB is therefore not buying the same module more slowly, it is a different topology — which is a reason to sample it, not a reason to refuse it."
